1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,210
Forty observations of pure noise. Nothing in them can be predicted.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:09,082
Yet a polynomial in time, of degree zero, one, two, up to six,

3
00:00:09,182 --> 00:00:14,831
fits them better and better. The in-sample error falls with every degree.

4
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,208
Then come five new observations: the exam the model did not see.

5
00:00:20,308 --> 00:00:24,823
The polynomial leaves the data behind and shoots off the chart. The

6
00:00:24,923 --> 00:00:28,887
best forecast of noise was the flat line at zero all along.

7
00:00:31,666 --> 00:00:34,450
Today: how to grade forecasts fairly, and how

8
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:37,783
to tell whether one forecaster really beats another.

9
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:42,915
Repeat this on a thousand samples and the pattern becomes a law.

10
00:00:43,015 --> 00:00:47,388
Each extra degree lowers the in-sample error a little, and raises the

11
00:00:47,488 --> 00:00:51,148
forecast error a lot, until it breaks through the ceiling.

12
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:56,801
Even one redundant parameter, a constant fitted to noise, costs sigma squared

13
00:00:56,901 --> 00:01:01,513
over T. In-sample fit measures how well a model explains what it

14
00:01:01,613 --> 00:01:05,709
has seen. Only data it has not seen can grade a forecast.

15
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,278
Split the sample: the first R observations to estimate, the

16
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:14,595
remaining P to forecast, one step at a time. How

17
00:01:14,695 --> 00:01:18,328
the estimation window moves is the choice of a camera.

18
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,134
The fixed camera never moves. The rolling camera

19
00:01:21,234 --> 00:01:23,909
keeps its width and slides along. The recursive

20
00:01:24,009 --> 00:01:26,685
camera stays anchored at the start, and widens.

21
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:29,763
Now let the world change in the middle of the exam,

22
00:01:29,863 --> 00:01:33,833
and draw each camera's forecast. The rolling window forgets the old

23
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:38,023
regime and catches up. The recursive window averages across the break

24
00:01:38,123 --> 00:01:41,060
and lags behind. The fixed window never finds out.

25
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:44,615
Neither moving camera wins everywhere. Rolling buys

26
00:01:44,715 --> 00:01:48,479
robustness to breaks with fewer observations; recursive

27
00:01:48,579 --> 00:01:51,430
buys precision, if the world stands still.

28
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,830
Two forecasters, A and B, sit the same exam. After each

29
00:01:55,930 --> 00:01:59,759
question we record the difference of their squared errors, d t.

30
00:01:59,859 --> 00:02:02,378
Below the line, A did better; above it, B.

31
00:02:02,466 --> 00:02:06,492
The Diebold Mariano statistic is a running tally: the average

32
00:02:06,592 --> 00:02:11,159
difference, times root P, divided by its long-run standard deviation.

33
00:02:11,259 --> 00:02:15,082
Forecast errors echo one another, so omega is the long-run

34
00:02:15,182 --> 00:02:18,667
variance of lecture two, estimated by Newey and West.

35
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:22,835
Early on, the tally wanders inside the band of plus or minus

36
00:02:22,935 --> 00:02:26,587
one point nine six: the exam is too short to tell them

37
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:31,798
apart. As questions accumulate, A's small edge stops looking like luck, and

38
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:35,411
the tally crosses minus one point nine six for good.

39
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,775
So: grade a forecaster only on the exam it did not see.

40
00:02:39,875 --> 00:02:43,213
And crown a winner only when the gap in scores is larger

41
00:02:43,313 --> 00:02:47,632
than luck, measured with the long-run variance of the loss differential.

